<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baltic Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Baltic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baltic Sea is an arm of the Atlantic in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the Baltic states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Control of its trade and coasts was the central aim of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> in the seventeenth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> exported grain through its ports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> access to the Baltic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baltic-sea.jpg" alt="Baltic Sea" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabriel Ziegler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Baltic_Sea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
